-reports/report/report-to-the-legislative-assembly-performance-audits/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General&apos;s Report was an update of a previous 2010 report and covered the period of April 2010 to July 2017. The Report was part of a collaboration involving most provincial legislative audit offices and the Auditor General of Canada and sought to determine the extent to which federal, provincial and territorial governments were meeting commitments to reduce GHG emissions and adapting to climate change.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb2ef705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Perspectives on Climate Change Action in Canada (March 2018), at p. 3</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb2ef715f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/perspectives-on-climate-change-action-in-canada/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">According to the Auditor General&apos;s Report, although the Department of Sustainable Development was aware by the fall of 2009 that Manitoba was not going to meet its legislated 2012 target, the Department did not update its plan until December 2015.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb316805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.3</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb316815f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). Note that the Department of Sustainable Development is now the Department of Environment, Climate and Parks. For further discussion see this chapter at <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb316825f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:8">&sect;8:8</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General further criticised the December 2015 Plan as being too &ldquo;high-level&rdquo; and lacking in sufficient detail, analysis (including scientific and economic), inter-departmental monitoring and reporting to comprehensively set and tackle emissions targets.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb33d905f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at pp. 3-4, 18-20</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb33d915f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General also found that the Department&apos;s 2011 initiative to create a three-phase provincial plan for adapting to climate change impacts had not progressed across government departments.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb33d925f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at pp. 4-5, 22-23</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb364a05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> However, the Auditor General noted that despite a lack of coordination, the Department of Sustainable Development along with other government departments and agencies had nonetheless commenced addressing risks and opportunities associated with impacts (discussed later in <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb364a15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:18">&sect;8:18</ref></cite> of this chapter).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at pp. 4-5, 23-28, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb38bb05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In July 2017, the federal government and all provinces and territories with the exception of Manitoba and Saskatchewan signed the <ital>Pan-Canadian Framework on Clean Growth and Climate Change</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>Pan-Canadian Framework</ital>&rdquo;) which was designed to achieve Canada&apos;s commitment under the 2015 Paris Agreement.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Canada, Pan-Canadian Framework on Clean Growth and Climate Change, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb3b2c05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.canada.ca/en/services/environment/weather/climatechange/pan-canadian-framework.html</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Under the Paris Agreement, Canada committed to reducing its emissions to 30&percnt; below 2005 levels by 2030 and to take steps for adapting to climate change impacts.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Canada ratified the Paris Agreement on October 5th, 2016 and it entered into force on November 4th, 2016.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba&apos;s initial refusal to sign the <ital>Pan-Canadian Framework</ital> related to Manitoba&apos;s objection to the federal government&apos;s carbon pricing plan (discussed further in <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:18">&sect;8:18</ref></cite> and <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:19">&sect;8:19</ref></cite> of this chapter).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For additional discussion see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.10</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb400e05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Note that in April 2021, the Prime Minister of Canada announced a new national emissions reduction target <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;13" nep=";;15" hyphenated="n" folio="8-13" backFolio="8-14" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>of 40-45&percnt; below 2005 levels by 2030.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb400e15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Canada, Prime Minister Trudeau Announces Increased Climate Ambition (April 22, 2021)</ref></cite> available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb400e25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://pm.gc.ca/en/news/news-releases/2021/04/22/prime-minister-trudeau-announces-increased-climate-ambition</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> This was followed by the federal <ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital> which received Royal Assent in June 2021 and enshrined in legislation, the new 2030 reduction target as Canada&apos;s Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) under the Paris Agreement as well as a commitment to revise the national target every five years in order to achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions in Canada by 2050.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital>, S.C. 2021, c. 22 at ss. 1.1(2), 4 and 6.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><